#{[17592186541078 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541078 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541078 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541078 #edb/ref 1071 "The tangent line to the eagle's flight path is horizontal at $t=${{field-1}}$\\,\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4086d61d-9c18-5ba7-b425-e5509f913d48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541078 #edb/ref 1074 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $-\\sqrt{2}\\notin (0,\\infty ),$ so we only consider the value $t=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{5}{27}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the curve's tangent is horizontal when $t=\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541096 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541096 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541096 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541096 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the horizontal velocity of the eagle as a function of time.\n\nThe horizontal velocity of the bird is$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541096 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e20ee7e-741b-5069-a51c-7de25cc32da7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541096 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal velocity of the eagle is given by ${x}^{′}(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{t}{5}).\n$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{t}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(t/5)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}/25}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal velocity of the eagle is given by the function ${x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541100 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541100 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541100 #edb/ref 1071 "At time $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ the eagle is located at a point on $C$ with $y$-coordinate equal to $\\frac{95}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ Use the tangent line approximation to approximate the $y$-coordinate of the eagle's location when $t=\\frac{110}{23}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$y(\\frac{110}{23})≈$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "742d438b-e3dc-52b0-a068-888e70c6be15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541100 #edb/ref 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $y(t)$ at the point $a=5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =y(5)+{y}^{′}(5)(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3},$ and since ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2,$ then ${y}^{′}(5)=25-2=23.$ \n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $y(\\frac{110}{23})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{110}{23}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{110}{23}) & ≈L(\\frac{110}{23}) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(\\frac{110}{23}-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(-\\frac{5}{23}) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{3}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541108 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541108 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541108 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $y(t),$ the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t).$\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541108 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "836e24be-b58e-500b-bb05-e9b2030ce5e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541108 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {t}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2\\int {t}^{0}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2+1}}{2+1})-2t+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541112 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541112 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133286" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541112 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541112 #edb/ref 1071 "The vertical velocity of the eagle increases at a rate proportional to the square of the time $t.$ In addition, the eagle loses $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ in the positive $y$-direction due to wind resistance. It is known that the eagle's vertical velocity equals $2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe differential equation that describes the eagle's vertical velocity is ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541112 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95631ad4-7573-5741-9c54-4f854208188c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541112 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertical velocity increases proportionally with ${t}^{2}$ and decreases by $2.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=k{t}^{2}-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality.\n\nSince ${y}^{′}(2)=2,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(2) & =2 \\\\ k(2)^{2}-2 & =2 \\\\ 4k & =4 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541128 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541128 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541128 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541128 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541128 #edb/ref 1071 "${r}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=\\,$$⟨$\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$⟩$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541128 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1baae25-37ff-504a-9b40-31c45e15f352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541128 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n* For the $x$-component, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=\\frac{5}{25+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\n* For the $y$-component, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=(\\sqrt{5})^{2}-2=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\sqrt{5}) & =⟨{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{5}),{y}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{6},3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541148 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541148 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541148 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541148 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541148 #edb/ref 1071 "The eagle is descending for all $t$ in the interval$\\,t\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541148 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb009f1b-54cb-513a-ae27-a2c22d910e4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541148 #edb/ref 1074 "If the eagle is descending, then its vertical velocity is negative.\n\nWe know that the eagle's vertical velocity is given by ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nTo calculate the time interval in which the eagle's vertical velocity is decreasing, we solve the inequality ${y}^{′}(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & <0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2 & <0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & <2 \\\\ |t| & <\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $t>0.$ Therefore, the eagle is descending for $t\\in (0,\\sqrt{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541151 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541151 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541151 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541151 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ be the position vector of the eagle. Find the distance between the eagle and its nest at time $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe eagle is approximately$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$ away from its nest after $t=5$ seconds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541151 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4a5e43e-e7a6-55b0-a709-f89ec3a86933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541151 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the nest is at the origin, the distance between the bird and its nest at time $t=5$ is $|r(5)|.$\n\nWe know that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3}.$ Evaluating the function at $t=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(5) & =⟨\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{5}),\\frac{95}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{95}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(5)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{95}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9\\,025}{9}} \\\\  & ≈31.68\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541158 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186541175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541158 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b6a8ccd-d70c-5f07-bd06-c1a67bb8c918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541158 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186541063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541159 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186541225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541159 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e869152-61a2-5f4c-b677-eb10be0bff89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541159 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186541058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541165 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186541193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541165 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25ed461a-1ae1-5bcc-a645-a1adcac020ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541165 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186541100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541175 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186541218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541175 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39c190e2-19cb-57ce-9a89-1a038703f741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541175 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186541151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541181 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "539bd2dd-3cda-521c-a46e-f9eef18ef15e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541181 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186541052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541186 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186541159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541186 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5cd9358c-320d-5663-953d-407f8219b7ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541186 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186541112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541193 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186541158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541193 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "780c444c-203b-507b-affb-974182c4ecb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541193 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186541108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541207 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186541186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541207 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e4e6674-5e6f-501b-bec3-9bdb487d5059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541207 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186541096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541218 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c41b1b6f-713f-5c06-8fe9-5de055592653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541218 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186541128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541223 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186541165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541223 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6e7506e-0ef6-58fd-a0a5-b890fb1061ce" 